# Combat-PoD Duel Builder Guide

This guide covers how to start a combat-minigame fight from script --
the create / add / finalize builder.

For the system internals (variable namespace, slot lifecycle, execution
phases, result codes), see `COMBAT_REFERENCE.info`. This guide focuses on
the authoring workflow: how to assemble a roster and kick off a fight.

The builder effects live in
`devpod/common/scripted_effects/POD_combat/pod_combat_group_effects.txt`.

---

## Do you even need the builder?

Most duels should NOT call the builder directly.

PoD's whole duel system (`hold_court`, schemes, activity events, the
`single_combat.*` chain, etc.) routes through
`configure_start_single_combat_effect`. the combat-minigame overrides that
effect (in `PoD_override_single_combat_effects.txt`) and, for any
PC-involved fight, automatically builds a **1v1** for you -- create + add
the opponent + finalize, with the full caller context (follow-up event,
parent-event scope aliases, rule flags) preserved.

So if you're writing a normal story duel, just call
`configure_start_single_combat_effect` the way PoD always has. The
minigame takes over for free.

**Call the builder directly only when you want a custom roster** the
single-combat path can't express -- group fights, hand-picked
combatants, ally/enemy line-ups that aren't "PC vs one opponent." The
debug interactions in `POD_debug_interactions.txt` are the worked
reference for this; the 1vN / Nv1 / NvN ones build their rosters by hand.

---

## The builder at a glance

Three steps, five effects:

```
<PC scope> = {
    POD_combat_create_duel_effect    = { ...config... }   # 1. once
    POD_combat_duel_add_ally_effect  = { CHARACTER = X }  # 2. any number
    POD_combat_duel_add_enemy_effect = { CHARACTER = X }  # 2. 1+ (>=1 enemy)
    POD_combat_finalize_duel_effect  = yes                # 3. once
}
```

| Effect | When | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `POD_combat_create_duel_effect` | once, first | Scaffolds host state, creates the duel story (saved as `scope:pod_combat_duel`), seats the PC at **player team, position 0** |
| `POD_combat_duel_add_ally_effect` | any number | Appends a character at the next free **player** position (1, 2, 3, …) and bumps `pod_combat_player_team_size` |
| `POD_combat_duel_add_enemy_effect` | 1+ times | Appends a character at the next free **enemy** position (0, 1, 2, …) and bumps `pod_combat_enemy_team_size`; the **first** enemy goes to enemy position 0 |
| `POD_combat_finalize_duel_effect` | once, last | Inits every slot, computes hit chances, opens the combat window, starts round 1 |

The roster shape: **PC = player/0**, allies = player/1, player/2, …;
primary opponent = enemy/0, reinforcements = enemy/1, enemy/2, …. Every
seat carries an explicit `combat_team` flag (`flag:player` / `flag:enemy`)
and a `team_position` (an int 0+, position 0 = the team leader) -- team is
not inferred from a single slot index, and there is no "mode" variable.
Teams can be arbitrarily large; there is no per-side cap (see rule 5).

---

## The golden rules

1. **Run everything in the PC's scope.** The PC is the combat host --
   all the state hangs off them, and they are player/0. If your `on_accept`
   has `scope:actor = <the PC>`, wrap the builder in `scope:actor = { ... }`.

2. **Never add the PC.** `create_duel` already seats them at player/0.
   Adding them again would burn an ally slot on a duplicate.

3. **The first enemy you add is the "primary opponent" (enemy/0).** It's
   the hit-chance baseline for the whole player team and the PC's default
   target. Add the headline enemy first; reinforcements after.

4. **Pass SAVED SCOPES to `CHARACTER`, never a `var:` chain.** `add_*`
   (and the init pass behind finalize) re-scope onto the character, so a
   scope-relative `var:foo.var:bar` would resolve against the wrong scope
   and throw. Use `save_scope_as` (or an already-saved scope like
   `scope:recipient`) and pass that.

5. **No per-side cap -- teams are arbitrarily large.** Each `add_*` call
   appends a slot at the next free position and increments a per-team
   counter (`pod_combat_player_team_size` / `pod_combat_enemy_team_size`).
   Call them as many times as you like; nothing is dropped. The only bound
   is a soft guardrail constant, `pod_combat_max_team_size` (default 50,
   trivially raised), there to catch a runaway loop -- it is not a design
   cap. (Visuals strain at very large counts, but the engine handles any N.)

6. **`finalize` is what opens the window.** Until you call it,
   `pod_combat_active` is not set and the GUI stays hidden. Build the
   whole roster first, finalize last.

---

## Gathering a roster from a loop

When you pull combatants from `every_relation` / `every_knight` / etc.,
the iterator's scope is the candidate, but `add_*` must run in the PC
(host) scope. So: save the candidate, then re-enter the PC scope to add.

```
scope:actor = {                          # PC / host scope
    every_relation = {
        type = coterie_member
        limit = { is_alive = yes is_adult = yes NOT = { has_trait = incapable } }
        save_scope_as = duel_ally        # iterator scope = the ally
        scope:actor = {                  # back into host scope
            POD_combat_duel_add_ally_effect = { CHARACTER = scope:duel_ally }
        }
    }
}
```

`scope:actor` is still resolvable inside the loop because saved scopes
stay visible in nested blocks.

---

## `create_duel` parameters

```
POD_combat_create_duel_effect = {
    INITIATOR    = scope:actor       # who started the duel
    ATTACKER     = scope:actor       # the attacking party (PoD sc_attacker)
    DEFENDER     = scope:recipient   # the defending party (PoD sc_defender)
    FATALITY     = possible          # flag -- see table below
    FIXED        = no                # flag -- no for the minigame
    LOCALE       = terrain_scope     # flag -- background scene
    OUTPUT_EVENT = my_namespace.0001 # event fired on INITIATOR at combat end
}
```

`INITIATOR` / `ATTACKER` / `DEFENDER` are the PoD duel parties, in the
`sc_*` sense -- they are **independent of slot layout**. The PC can be the
defender (seated at player/0) while the attacker sits at enemy/0. The post-combat
event (`pod_combat.9999`) restores these so win/loss attribution and any
`scope:sc_victor` logic in your follow-up stay correct. For a fight the
PC instigates, all three are usually the PC / the PC / the opponent.

### FATALITY modes

| Value | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `no` | Non-fatal. Wounds are written live but capped so no track fills -- nobody dies. |
| `practice` | Sparring. Real wounds land live and heals work, but the fight is unkillable; each combatant's wound track is snapshot at start and restored at the end, and no lasting Mental Strain is applied. |
| `possible` | Real fight. Wounds land; death from a filled wound track is possible. |
| `default` | PoD's default: tribal duels are to the death, otherwise like `possible`. |
| `always` | The loser is killed outright at the end. |

### LOCALE

A background-scene flag (`battlefield`, `terrain_scope`, `throne_room`,
`alley_night`, ...). `terrain_scope` uses the defender's location. It only
affects presentation; pick whatever matches the fiction.

### OUTPUT_EVENT

The event fired on `INITIATOR` when combat ends, via `pod_combat.9999`.
Use it to react to the result -- read `scope:sc_victor` / `scope:sc_loser`
in your event. Parent-event scope aliases you saved before launching
(`scope:spirit`, `scope:scheme`, `scope:vassal_claim`, ...) are stashed
automatically and restored before your event fires, so they're available
there too.

